---
import RevealLayout from "../../layouts/PresentationLayout.astro";
import {
  SeaViews as SeaViewsViz,
  SeaViewsSolver,
  ComplexityMatrix,
} from "../../components/cave/buildings-with-ocean-view";
import RevealCode from "../../components/RevealCode";
import { LogoAnimation } from "../../components/cave/intro";
import katex from "katex";
import "katex/dist/katex.min.css"; // Importa los estilos

const formula = "\\mathcal{O}(n^2)";
const katex_n2 = katex.renderToString(formula, { throwOnError: false });
---

<RevealLayout
  title="Buildings With an Ocean View"
  description="From naive comparisons to an elegant one-pass solution, visualized step by step"
  theme="dark"
>
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Many people dream of living by the sea. But being close does not always
        mean having a view. This building is only a few steps from the beach.
        Yet it will never see the ocean. The problem is not distance. The
        problem is height.
      </p>
      <pre>Highlight building in index 2</pre>
    </aside>
    Problem introduction with a visualization of buildings and ocean view.
  </section>

  <section id="logo">
    <LogoAnimation client:load />
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        A building has an ocean view if every building in front of it, toward
        the beach, is strictly shorter. If there is one building that is equal
        or taller, the view is blocked. That's all.
      </p>
      <p>
        So how do we check this in a systematic way? Suppose we want to know
        whether a single building has an ocean view. Let's pick the one furthest
        from the ocean. It has height four. In front of it, toward the ocean, we
        see buildings with heights one, three, and two. All of them are shorter.
        So this building has a view.
      </p>
      <p>
        Now try another one. This building has height two. In front of it we see
        one and three. Three is taller than two. So this building is blocked.
        Just one building that is taller or equal, and the view is gone.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <SeaViewsViz
      client:load
      heights={[1, 3, 2, 4]}
      showEditor={false}
      width={1080}
      height={580}
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        That works. But what if we want to know which ones have a view? We don't
        just want to check one. We want to find all of them. We can repeat that
        same check for every position. Then repeat the same process again and
        again.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <SeaViewsViz
      client:load
      heights={[1, 3, 2, 4]}
      showEditor={false}
      width={1080}
      height={580}
    />
  </section>

  <section id="matrix">
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Let's make this visual. We draw a matrix. Each column is a building.
        Each row is a comparison with a building closer to the ocean. Every time
        we compare two buildings, we place a dot. The building closest to the
        ocean compares with no one.
      </p>
      <p>
        The next compares with one. We compare with the first one, so we place a
        dot in the first row. Then we move to the next building. Now there are
        two buildings in front of it, so we compare with both of them. That
        fills two rows. Then the next building compares with three. And this
        keeps growing.
      </p>
      <p>
        If we call n the number of buildings, then this pattern forms roughly
        half of an n by n grid. That's why the running time grows quadratically.
        It works. But it doesn't scale well.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <ComplexityMatrix
      client:load
      heights={[1, 3, 2, 4]}
      showEditor={true}
      width={1080}
      height={580}
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <span class="text-7xl" set:html={katex_n2} />
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Let's try a couple of small examples and see what we can learn. Take: 3,
        1, 2. The first one is already the tallest. So none of the ones behind
        it can have a view.
      </p>
      <pre>3, 1, 2</pre>
      <p>
        The only way something behind can get a view is if it becomes a new
        record. So if we add a 3 nothing changes. In real life you might argue
        it has a view... but in our problem definition, it doesn't. It's still
        blocked by the first building.
      </p>
      <pre>3, 1, 2, 3</pre>
      <p>
        If we add a 4 at the end, because 4 beats the maximum so far, it has a
        view. So here is the idea: as we move away from the ocean, we keep track
        of the tallest height up to this point. If the current building beats
        that value, it has a view, and we update the maximum.
      </p>
      <pre>3, 1, 2, 3, 4</pre>
      <pre>3, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 3, 6</pre>
    </aside>
    <SeaViewsSolver
      client:load
      heights={[3, 1, 2]}
      showEditor={true}
      showControls={true}
      width={1080}
      height={660}
    />
  </section>

  <section id="code" class="text-lg">
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Let's implement this in Python. First, we prepare a list to store the
        buildings that have a view. We also keep a variable called "max so far".
        This represents the tallest building we have seen up to this point. We
        start with a sentinel value of -1, lower than any valid height, so the
        first building always counts as a new maximum.
      </p>
      <p>
        Now we scan the buildings from left to right. Remember, the ocean is on
        the left. For each height, we check one condition: is this building
        taller than the maximum we have seen so far?
      </p>
      <p>
        If it is, then it has a view. So we record its position. And we update
        the maximum, because this is now the tallest building. At the end, we
        simply return the result.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <RevealCode
      client:only="react"
      animation="fade"
      code={`def ocean_views(heights: list[int]) -> list[int]:
    # STEP-1
    result = []
    # STEP-2
    max_so_far = -1  # heights are assumed to be >= 0

    # STEP-3
    for i, h in enumerate(heights):
        # STEP-4
        if h > max_so_far:  # strictly taller than everything to the left
            # STEP-5
            result.append(i)
            # STEP-6
            max_so_far = h

    # STEP-7
    return result`}
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        So the whole problem comes down to a single idea: keep the maximum
        height seen so far. Here's something you can try next. What changes if
        the ocean is on the right instead of the left? And what if there is
        ocean on both sides?
      </p>
      <p>
        This was today's cheese byte. Remember, the code matters but the idea
        comes first.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </section>
</RevealLayout>
